2887 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Parameter 1\072 Category Clarity\n\nThe question being answered\072 \042What is this product\077\042\n\nThis is the first thing a visitor\047s brain tries to find answer to\056 If they can\047t place the product in a mental category within first 5 seconds\054 they leave\054 not because they\047re uninterested\054 but because the cognitive load is too high\056\n\nWhat to look for\072\n\nDoes the hero headline or sub\055headline name a recognisable product category\077 \050e\056g\056\054 \042revenue intelligence platform\042\054 \042AP automation software\042\051\nDoes the category language match how buyers actually search and describe the problem or is it some internally invented category name\057jargon\077\nIs the category claim too broad \050\042AI\055powered platform for teams\042\051 or too narrow \050\042Jira automation for fintech ops teams with 500\053 engineers\042\051\077\n\nScoring guide\072\n\n5\072 Category is named explicitly\054 matches buyer language\054 is appropriately scoped\n4\072 Category is implied but requires a sentence of reading to confirm\n3\072 Category is buried in a subheadline or requires scrolling\n2\072 Category is replaced by a vague aspiration \050\042the future of work\042 type language\051\n1\072 No category signal at all and the page reads like internal documentation\n\nFailure pattern\072\n \nFounder\055written headlines that lead with the company\047s unique methodology rather than the category \050e\056g\056 \042The Compound Growth Operating System\042 instead of \042Revenue Analytics Software\042\051\056\n\nFill your Score \072)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 2888 0 R
     /Pg 484 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

